<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Indian Army</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Indian Army was the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>, recruited largely from Indian soldiers under British officers. It policed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and served in campaigns across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, including both world wars. It was one of the main military pillars of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The economic relationship with India was especially consequential: Indian revenues paid for the <b>Indian Army</b> — the empire's largest standing force — while India served both as a major market for Lancashire textiles and as the keystone of Britain's position in the Indian Ocean.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-empire">British Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Indian Army</b> of the Second World War, about 2.5 million strong, is often described as the largest all-volunteer army in history.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-raj">British Raj · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>India inherited a large share of the British <b>Indian Army</b> and has since maintained one of the biggest armed forces in the world, with well over a million active personnel.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-india">Republic of India · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the covenants of merger these units passed to the union and were then taken over by the Government of India, which absorbed the State Forces into the <b>Indian Army</b> by 1951.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-state-of-rajasthan">United State of Rajasthan · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Sikh soldiers served in large numbers in the British <b>Indian Army</b> in both world wars, and Sikhs have played a prominent part in the politics, agriculture and economy of modern India.</blockquote><a href="/religion/sikhism">Sikhism · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Its rulers promoted the lean Mudhol hound, a sight-hound now used by the <b>Indian Army</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/mudhol-state">Mudhol State</a></li></ul></section>
